function episodeDetailEn(ep){ const [num,title,phase]=ep; const hook=episodeHooksEn[num-1]; const isFirst=num===1; return {
 status:isFirst?'First Script Draft':'Story Outline',duration:'4–7 minutes',scene:num<=15?'James’s living room / bedroom entrance':'Living room + essential exterior',cast:'James, Angeline, Joseph',hook,
 five:`Within the first 30 seconds, establish the characters, the episode event and all three positions so the audience understands why the situation will keep escalating.`,
 goal:`Angeline tries to solve “${title}” in her own way while protecting her image as wealthy, independent, and never wrong.`,
 obstacle:`James insists on Singapore rules, tenancy logic, or property management; Joseph turns the conflict into a content opportunity and makes it worse.`,
 mid:`Act Two must contain at least two failed attempts. First make the problem appear easy to solve, then reveal that the real obstacle is a rule, pride, misunderstanding or the trio’s relationship.`,
 climax:`In Act Three, all three approaches collide in a complete climax, consequence and relationship shift. The reversal must pay off the title rather than end with only one joke.`,
 ending:`End on one short reversal and a comment question: who was actually more reasonable?`,
 culture:`Use only one genuine Singapore-life point connected to “${title}”. Verify any policy, fee, or legal claim before publishing.`,
 script:isFirst?[
 '【Shot 1 / Wide at entrance】Angeline enters with several suitcases and looks around.','Angeline: Where is my room?','James points to a small door: Here.','Angeline: I asked for my room, not the storeroom.','James: That is your room.','【Shot 2 / Phone close-up】Angeline compares the listing photos.','Angeline: It was much bigger in the photos.','James: The photos did not lie. It was a wide-angle lens.','Joseph raises his phone: That reaction was good. Say it again—I missed it.','Angeline: I want to cancel the lease.','James: Early termination means no deposit refund.','【Pause + three reaction shots】','Angeline: Actually… a smaller room feels safer.'
 ]:[`[Act One · Scene 1 / Opening] ${hook}`,'[Act One · Scene 2] Establish the event of the day, the relationships and why this problem must be dealt with now.','[Act One · Scene 3] Angeline proposes a seemingly effective solution; James objects from a landlord or local-rules perspective; Joseph sees a content opportunity.','[Act One Turn] A new condition appears, preventing anyone from simply walking away.','[Act Two · Scene 4] First attempt: Angeline takes control but fails because of overconfidence or unfamiliarity with local life.','[Act Two · Scene 5] James takes over with the safe solution, which Angeline or Joseph disrupts.','[Act Two · Scene 6] Joseph intervenes for reach, advertising or footage. It appears to work briefly, then creates a larger consequence.','[Midpoint Reversal] They realise the real problem is not money but a rule, pride, misunderstanding or lack of trust.','[Act Two Back Half] The three blame one another. An older tension resurfaces and one character exposes a genuine weakness.','[Low Point] The plan fails completely and now affects the property, a client, school or their relationship.','[Act Three · Scene 7] They are forced to cooperate, with each character contributing a genuine strength.','[Climax] Pay off the episode title through action, dialogue and a relationship reversal—not only a punchline.','[Consequence] Resolve the main problem or leave a believable cost, showing what changed.','[Tag Scene] Return to normal life, then introduce a small new conflict or next-episode thread.','[End Gag] Add a 10–20 second character beat that can also be cut into a promotional clip.']
};}
